1
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:11,074
How can RM8,000 give you exposure to a contract worth RM100,000?

2
00:00:11,814 --> 00:00:16,756
The answer is leverage. And it works for you, and against you, at the same speed.

3
00:00:18,656 --> 00:00:25,398
In this video, we unpack leverage: what it is, how it is calculated, and why it must be respected.

4
00:00:28,098 --> 00:00:39,640
Start with contract value. FCPO at RM4,000 per tonne, times 25 tonnes, gives a contract value of RM100,000.

5
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:44,058
That is the size of the price exposure you carry with just one contract.

6
00:00:46,158 --> 00:00:56,284
But futures traders do not pay the full RM100,000 upfront. Instead, they deposit margin: collateral to support the position.

7
00:00:56,624 --> 00:01:06,030
In this example, assume an initial margin of RM8,000. This is a hypothetical figure for education, not a current margin rate.

8
00:01:06,370 --> 00:01:14,456
Margin is not a down payment and not the price of the contract. You do not own RM100,000 of palm oil.

9
00:01:16,556 --> 00:01:26,874
Leverage compares your exposure with your capital. RM100,000 divided by RM8,000 is 12.5 times.

10
00:01:27,214 --> 00:01:38,372
In other words, every RM1 of margin supports about RM12.50 of contract exposure. Leverage is not free money; it is borrowed exposure to price risk.

11
00:01:40,472 --> 00:01:48,654
Now see what a small price move does. A RM40 move against you is only 1% of the RM4,000 price.

12
00:01:48,994 --> 00:01:54,992
But RM40 times 25 is a RM1,000 loss per contract.

13
00:01:55,332 --> 00:02:06,946
And RM1,000 is 12.5% of the RM8,000 margin. A 1% price move becomes a 12.5% change in your margin.

14
00:02:07,286 --> 00:02:13,740
It works the same way in your favour, which is exactly why leverage feels attractive, and why it is dangerous.

15
00:02:15,840 --> 00:02:24,022
If losses keep reducing your account equity below the required level, the broker may issue a margin call, asking you to top up funds.

16
00:02:24,362 --> 00:02:33,840
If the funds are not provided in time, or the market moves very fast, positions may be force-liquidated, meaning closed, according to the broker's terms.

17
00:02:34,180 --> 00:02:39,650
And losses can exceed the margin you deposited. You remain responsible for any shortfall.

18
00:02:41,750 --> 00:02:45,564
That is why risk management is essential when trading with leverage.

19
00:02:45,904 --> 00:02:51,350
Know the full contract value, not just the margin. Size your positions to your capital.

20
00:02:51,690 --> 00:02:56,608
Plan your exit before you enter, and keep enough buffer in your account for price swings.

21
00:02:58,708 --> 00:03:08,090
Contract value is price times contract size: for FCPO at RM4,000, that is RM100,000.

22
00:03:08,430 --> 00:03:11,836
Margin is collateral, not the price of the contract.

23
00:03:12,176 --> 00:03:17,118
Leverage compares exposure with margin: here, 12.5 times.

24
00:03:17,458 --> 00:03:21,032
A small price move creates a much larger change in your margin.

25
00:03:21,372 --> 00:03:26,554
Losses can trigger a margin call or forced liquidation, and can exceed your margin.

26
00:03:26,894 --> 00:03:28,812
Risk management comes first.

27
00:03:30,112 --> 00:03:37,238
Leverage can increase opportunity, but at the same time it accelerates losses. Understand the risks before you trade.

28
00:03:39,338 --> 00:03:43,104
This is only the foundation for understanding the futures market.

29
00:03:43,444 --> 00:03:46,442
Know the product you trade before taking any risk.

30
00:03:46,782 --> 00:03:49,852
Continue your learning with Capital Market Institute.

31
00:03:51,752 --> 00:03:57,414
The references for this video are shown on screen. Always check the latest official specifications.

32
